  "content": "<h2>Why Blast Radius Containment Is an Architectural Decision, Not an Afterthought</h2><p>When a single agent in a multi-agent pipeline fails, the question is never just 'why did it fail?' The question is 'how far did that failure travel?' In production systems running dozens of concurrent agent types across multiple departments, an uncontained failure cascades faster than any human operator can intervene. By the time you see the alert, three downstream agents have already consumed bad state, a Kafka consumer group has stalled, and your pipeline has quietly stopped making progress.</p><p>Blast radius containment is the architectural discipline of ensuring that when something fails — and it will fail — the damage stays local. This is not about preventing failure. It is about designing systems where failure is survivable without halting the entire pipeline.</p>

<h2>The Failure Modes That Actually Appear in Production</h2><p>Most multi-agent systems that fail in production do not fail because of a single catastrophic bug. They fail because of compounding partial failures that the architecture was not designed to contain. The patterns are consistent:</p><ul><li><strong>Agent poison loops:</strong> An agent receives a malformed message, fails to process it, and the message is requeued. Without a dead-letter mechanism, the agent loops indefinitely, consuming thread capacity and blocking downstream consumers.</li><li><strong>State corruption propagation:</strong> An agent writes partial state to Postgres before crashing. A downstream agent reads that partial state and proceeds, producing outputs based on corrupt input. By the time the corruption is detected, it has touched multiple pipeline stages.</li><li><strong>Consumer group starvation:</strong> A slow or hung agent holds a Kafka partition lease, preventing other consumers in the group from making progress. The partition falls behind, lag accumulates, and dependent agents starve waiting for messages that are stuck behind the hung consumer.</li><li><strong>Supervisor cascade failure:</strong> In a supervisor-worker topology, a supervisor agent that manages retries and escalations becomes a single point of failure. If the supervisor crashes while workers are mid-task, the workers complete their work but have no mechanism to report results or receive new assignments.</li><li><strong>Memory pressure cross-contamination:</strong> Agents running in shared pods without resource limits compete for memory. A single agent with a memory leak degrades the performance of every other agent in the same pod, creating a failure mode that presents as intermittent slowness rather than a clean crash.</li></ul>

<h2>Kubernetes Pod Isolation: The First Line of Containment</h2><p>The most direct mechanism for blast radius containment at the infrastructure level is strict pod isolation in Kubernetes. Each agent type runs in its own pod with explicit CPU and memory limits defined in the resource spec. This is not optional configuration — it is the boundary that prevents one agent's resource consumption from affecting another.</p><p>In practice, this means defining both <code>requests</code> and <code>limits</code> for every agent container. Requests determine scheduling; limits enforce the ceiling. An agent that hits its memory limit gets OOMKilled cleanly rather than degrading its neighbours. That is a better outcome. A clean crash with a restart policy is recoverable. Silent degradation across a shared pod is not.</p><p>Beyond resource limits, pod disruption budgets control how many instances of a given agent type can be unavailable simultaneously during node maintenance or rolling updates. For high-throughput agents processing time-sensitive tasks, a PDB that allows zero voluntary disruptions ensures the Kubernetes scheduler does not inadvertently halve your processing capacity during a node drain.</p><p>Namespace-level network policies add another containment layer. Agents that have no legitimate reason to communicate directly with each other should not be able to. Lateral movement of a failure — where one compromised or misbehaving agent starts sending unexpected traffic to other agents — is a real production risk in complex topologies. Network policies enforce the communication graph at the infrastructure level, independent of application logic.</p>

<h2>Kafka Dead-Letter Queues and Consumer Group Isolation</h2><p>Kafka's durability guarantees are valuable precisely because they make it easy to reason about message delivery. But those same guarantees become a liability if you do not design for the messages that cannot be processed. A message that fails processing repeatedly will block consumer group progress if you allow unlimited requeuing without escalation.</p><p>The correct pattern is a dead-letter topic per agent type, with a configurable retry threshold before a message is routed to the DLT. The threshold depends on the failure mode you are protecting against: transient errors (network timeouts, downstream service unavailability) warrant several retries with exponential backoff; structural errors (malformed message schema, missing required fields) should fail fast to the DLT on the first attempt, because retrying will not fix a schema problem.</p><p>Consumer group isolation matters for blast radius containment in a different way. If multiple agent types share a consumer group on the same topic, a stalled consumer in one agent type affects the partition assignment for all agent types in that group. The correct architecture assigns each agent type its own consumer group, even when they are consuming from the same topic. This ensures that a stalled or slow agent in one group does not affect the lag or partition assignment of agents in other groups.</p><p>Partition count also has containment implications. A topic with a single partition serialises all consumption through a single consumer. If that consumer stalls, the entire topic stalls. Partitioning by a meaningful key — task type, tenant ID, priority tier — allows parallel consumption and limits the scope of a single consumer failure to the partitions it owns.</p>

<h2>Postgres State Management Under Concurrent Agent Load</h2><p>Agent state persistence in Postgres introduces a class of failure modes that are easy to miss in staging but appear reliably under production load: concurrent writes to the same state record from multiple agent instances.</p><p>Optimistic locking is the correct pattern for agent state machines. Each state record carries a version column. When an agent reads a record, it captures the version. When it writes back, it includes a <code>WHERE version = $captured_version</code> condition in the update. If another agent instance has modified the record between the read and the write, the update affects zero rows, and the writing agent knows it has a stale read. It can re-fetch and retry, or escalate to a supervisor depending on the retry semantics of the task.</p><p>This matters for blast radius containment because the alternative — last-write-wins with no version check — allows concurrent agents to silently overwrite each other's state. The pipeline continues processing, but on corrupted state. Optimistic locking converts a silent data corruption risk into a detectable conflict that the system can handle explicitly.</p><p>Schema design for agent state machines should encode the valid state transitions explicitly. A status column with an enum type and a check constraint that only allows valid transitions prevents an agent from writing an invalid state, even if application logic has a bug. Postgres enforces the constraint at the database level, independent of which agent instance is writing.</p><p>For high-contention state records — task queues, agent assignment tables, shared counters — advisory locks or <code>SELECT FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code> provide row-level locking without the deadlock risk of table-level locks. <code>SKIP LOCKED</code> is particularly useful for work queue patterns: each agent instance claims a row it can lock immediately and skips rows that are already locked by other instances, distributing work without coordination overhead.</p>

<h2>Circuit Breakers at the Agent Boundary</h2><p>When an agent depends on an external service — an LLM API, a data enrichment endpoint, an internal microservice — that dependency is a failure injection point. If the external service becomes slow or unavailable, the agent will accumulate in-flight requests, exhaust its connection pool, and eventually fail in ways that propagate upstream through the pipeline.</p><p>A circuit breaker at the agent boundary monitors the failure rate of calls to the external dependency. When the failure rate crosses a threshold, the circuit opens and the agent stops attempting calls, failing fast instead. This has two effects: it prevents the agent from accumulating blocked threads waiting for a service that is not responding, and it reduces load on the failing service, giving it space to recover.</p><p>The circuit breaker state machine — closed, open, half-open — needs to be externalised to a shared store (Redis or Postgres) if multiple instances of the same agent type are running. A circuit breaker that lives only in the memory of a single agent instance will not protect the other instances from making calls to a service that has already been identified as failing.</p>

